<MarketingLayout title="Seatview | Building an AI system around a revenue problem | Danny Sullivan" description="Inside Seatview: business priorities, OpenAI Agents API sessions, bounded execution, independent image review, spending safeguards, durable workflows, and human-controlled publication.">
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-hero"><a class="text-link" href="/">← Danny Sullivan / Applied AI leadership</a><p class="eyebrow">Gametime / Applied AI case study / November 2026</p><h1>The right view.<br />The right event.<br /><em>A system to get there.</em></h1><p class="case-deck">How I’m building Seatview to connect commercial priorities, AI image workflows, and human judgment—and what it takes to make that system dependable.</p><div class="case-meta"><div><span>My role</span><strong>Product Engineer · AI transformation</strong></div><div><span>Executive relationship</span><strong>Reporting directly to the CEO</strong></div><div><span>Scope</span><strong>Strategy, building, iteration, teaching</strong></div></div></header>
    <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Case study contents"><a href="#problem">The problem</a><a href="#value">Business value</a><a href="#system">The system</a><a href="#agents">Agents API</a><a href="#safeguards">Safeguards</a><a href="#leadership">Leadership</a><a href="#progress">Progress</a></nav>
    <section class="case-section" id="problem"><div class="chapter"><span>01</span><h2>A photo is part of<br />the purchase decision.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p class="intro-paragraph">A buyer isn’t purchasing “a ticket.” They are choosing a particular view of a particular event.</p><p>For Gametime, seat-view images sit close to that decision. A useful image helps someone understand the stage, the distance, the angle, and the obstructions. A missing image creates uncertainty. An appealing image from the wrong camera can create a different problem: confidence in a view the seat doesn’t offer.</p><p>The content problem grows in several dimensions at once: venues, sections, event types, changing stages, and seating configurations. A basketball photograph might provide a valid source camera for a concert transformation, but it does not establish the concert’s stage layout. Two floor sections with the same name can have different geometry in different event configurations.</p><p>Seatview brings coverage measurement, source discovery, generation, quality review, and publication into one operational system. The goal is useful, event-specific photo coverage with clear evidence behind each result.</p><blockquote>Preserve the buyer’s actual sightline. Change the event inside that view.</blockquote></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="value"><div class="chapter"><span>02</span><h2>Follow the business<br />value into the work.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p>I report directly to Gametime’s CEO and work across the distance between an AI opportunity and an operational product. For Seatview, that means focusing the queue on commercially relevant coverage gaps, rather than treating every missing image as equally valuable.</p><p>Upcoming music events are prioritized using realized transaction GMV. Exact event configurations connect the commercial data to section-level photo coverage. Dates sharing a configuration share a photo set, so additional event dates do not automatically multiply generation work.</p><div class="business-chain"><span>Realized event GMV</span><i>↓</i><span>Exact configuration + live sections</span><i>↓</i><span>Coverage gaps worth addressing</span><i>↓</i><span>Reviewed photos an operator can publish</span></div><p>This work touches the ticket-buying experience and the GMV associated with prioritized events. GMV measures gross merchandise value; it is different from Gametime’s revenue. GMV exposure identifies where the work matters, but it does not establish how much incremental revenue an image caused.</p><h3>Measure coverage and economics separately.</h3><div class="measurement-grid"><div><h4>Coverage</h4><p>The share of relevant live sections or tickets with usable seat-view imagery. Use the same inventory scope and time window when comparing changes.</p></div><div><h4>Quality</h4><p>Acceptance and correction rates, material visual failure categories, and operator decisions. A model pass alone is not proof of accuracy.</p></div><div><h4>Operating efficiency</h4><p>Cost per accepted image, attempts per section, time to review, and time to publication. Include failed attempts and incomplete usage.</p></div><div><h4>Commercial impact</h4><p>GMV associated with covered and uncovered inventory. Conversion or revenue uplift needs a separate measured attribution method.</p></div></div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="system"><div class="chapter"><span>03</span><h2>Explore the system,<br />one decision at a time.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p>Each stage has a distinct job, an explicit decision boundary, and a saved artifact. Select a stage to see how business priority turns into reviewable content.</p><div class="workflow-explorer" data-workflow><div class="workflow-selectors" aria-label="Workflow stages">{stages.map((stage, i) => <a href={`#stage-${stage.id}`} data-stage-link={stage.id}><span>0{i + 1}</span>{stage.label}<b aria-hidden="true">↗</b></a>)}</div><div class="workflow-panels">{stages.map((stage, i) => <section id={`stage-${stage.id}`} data-stage-panel={stage.id} aria-labelledby={`heading-${stage.id}`}><p class="eyebrow">0{i + 1} / {stage.label}</p><h3 id={`heading-${stage.id}`}>{stage.title}</h3><p>{stage.body}</p><div class="decision"><strong>The decision</strong><p>{stage.decision}</p></div><p class="artifact"><strong>Saved evidence</strong>{stage.artifact}</p></section>)}</div></div><p class="editorial-note">An explanatory walkthrough of the implemented workflow; this is not a live production dashboard.</p></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="agents"><div class="chapter"><span>04</span><h2>What the Agents API<br />actually does.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p class="intro-paragraph">The application owns the workflow. The agent receives a bounded task, specific evidence, and explicitly permitted operations.</p><p>Seatview includes a direct REST adapter for OpenAI’s Agents API. It creates sessions with instructions, visual inputs, a model and reasoning configuration, and a structured JSON output schema. Sessions run with no hosted execution environment; multi-agent spawning is disabled. The application supplies the frozen skill text rather than assuming the service can read the repository.</p><h3>1. Create and persist a session.</h3><p>The managed path creates a session for a defined planning, editing, or review task. It saves the session identity so the worker can inspect progress later. Creation and tool-result delivery use stable idempotency keys. The engine records creation intent before making the request, because a lost response is not evidence that the provider did nothing.</p><h3>2. Interpret explicit provider states.</h3><p>The adapter distinguishes working sessions, failures, requested actions, and completed turns. A completed session must contain a completed final answer for the relevant turn. Returned content is parsed against expected shapes; an empty answer or malformed assessment is a failure rather than an implicit success.</p><h3>3. Fulfill a narrowly defined tool call.</h3><p>The managed editing agent can request <code>edit_source</code>. The application validates the arguments and binds the image call to the original source, configuration, and rendering policy. It does not expose arbitrary shell access, database writes, or a publication tool. Unexpected or concurrent tool operations stop the task.</p><h3>4. Return the actual candidate and independent findings.</h3><p>The host saves the rendered artifact and runs a separate visual assessment. The tool result includes the candidate identity, the actual image, structured findings, and remaining attempts. The editor can correct a material failure within the remaining render allowance. Selection must resolve to a saved candidate that actually passed review.</p><h3>5. Resume from saved evidence.</h3><p>Temporal advances the engine through durable steps. Session IDs, tool calls, renders, and reviews survive worker restarts through application state. If delivery of a completed tool result is interrupted, the saved result can be delivered again without another image request. If a paid render’s outcome is unknown, the job stops for attention.</p><div class="architecture"><p class="eyebrow">Responsibilities / conceptual architecture</p><div><strong>Seatview workspace</strong><span>Priorities · comparison · operator decisions</span></div><i>↕</i><div><strong>Application + durable workers</strong><span>Scope · snapshots · budgets · leases · saved evidence</span></div><i>↕</i><div class="architecture-providers"><div><strong>Managed Agents API</strong><span>Saved session protocol</span></div><div><strong>Responses API</strong><span>New bounded visual checks</span></div><div><strong>Images API</strong><span>Application-controlled renders</span></div></div><p>Publication is a separate, explicitly confirmed application operation.</p></div><p class="editorial-note">API context: <a class="text-link" href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/agents-api/overview">Official OpenAI Agents API documentation ↗</a>. The controls described here are Seatview application decisions.</p><h3>The architecture changes as I learn.</h3><p>The latest Autopilot implementation moves new work toward direct application-controlled planning and rendering, with bounded, tool-free visual checks through the Responses API. The managed-session adapter remains available for saved sessions. This keeps the historical work inspectable while making new reasoning and rendering easier to constrain.</p><p>That is an important engineering choice: an agent loop should earn its complexity. Where a fixed sequence can provide the same useful judgment with clearer limits, I make that sequence explicit. I am continuing to evaluate the quality and spending tradeoffs on representative images.</p></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="safeguards"><div class="chapter"><span>05</span><h2>The practices that<br />make it operable.</h2><p>Cost, correctness, recovery, and human control are part of the product design.</p></div><div class="case-body"><p>These are concrete practices in the Seatview implementation. Expand each one for the failure it addresses, the control, and the operational lesson.</p><div class="practice-list">{practices.map((practice, i) => <details open={i === 0}><summary><span>{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span><h3>{practice.title}</h3><b aria-hidden="true">+</b></summary><div><p><strong>The failure to plan for.</strong> {practice.problem}</p><p>{practice.detail}</p><p class="practice-lesson">{practice.implication}</p></div></details>)}</div></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="leadership"><div class="chapter"><span>06</span><h2>Build the system.<br />Teach the judgment.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p class="intro-paragraph">My responsibility extends beyond Seatview. I provide company-wide guidance on AI transformation while reporting directly to the CEO.</p><p>I constantly test emerging capabilities, translate them into concrete opportunities, and teach people how to use them in their own work. Building gives the guidance substance: I can point to the actual constraints, costs, failure modes, and review decisions behind a working system.</p><p>Teaching informs the product in return. A teammate should be able to understand why a source was rejected, what a quality flag means, whether a run is still spending, and what approval will do. Those are interface decisions, but they are also decisions about trust and adoption.</p><h3>Experimentation is a continuous operating practice.</h3><p>For Seatview, iteration includes source-selection rules, camera and layout instructions, review tolerance, model settings, presentation, and spending controls. I inspect failures and change the appropriate layer. Sometimes the answer is a better instruction. Sometimes it is a better source. Sometimes it is a stricter application boundary or a simpler execution path.</p><p>That approach carries into broader AI guidance: start with real work, make a useful experiment, inspect its limits, teach what was learned, and decide what deserves further investment. The result should be both a better tool and a more capable team.</p></div></section>
    <section class="case-section" id="progress"><div class="chapter"><span>07</span><h2>What’s working.<br />What comes next.</h2></div><div class="case-body"><p>The core Seatview platform and the evolving Autopilot workflow have different evidence behind them. I keep that distinction explicit.</p><div class="status-row"><strong>Implemented</strong><p>Coverage and photo workflows; a managed-session adapter; bounded visual checks; durable job state; source and layout rules; independent review; spending reservations; operator review and publication guards.</p></div><div class="status-row"><strong>Locally exercised</strong><p>Recorded source analysis, generation, and QA pilots, alongside database and browser checks for workflow behavior. These demonstrate specific paths and outputs, not a universal image-quality score.</p></div><div class="status-row"><strong>Still to establish</strong><p>Representative quality and per-section cost evaluation on the latest bounded path, production verification for the evolving Autopilot implementation, and measured commercial uplift. Local pilots do not establish a production rollout.</p></div><h3>Data lifecycle is also an architecture decision.</h3><p>The system sends selected visual evidence to model providers and retains application evidence for review and recovery. A no-environment session is not a no-retention guarantee. OpenAI’s current Agents API documentation describes retained session state and says the endpoint does not support Zero Data Retention. Source rights, permitted inputs, retention, deletion, and access to saved artifacts need to be reviewed alongside quality and cost as deployment scope expands.</p><p>I want this work to be judged by the quality of its decisions and evidence. The business opportunity is meaningful. Demonstrating it responsibly means being clear about what the system does today, what I’m refining, and what still needs to be measured.</p><p class="editorial-note">Implementation account based on the Seatview repository and operator documentation available November 4, 2026. Technical details describe that implementation snapshot.</p></div></section>
  </article>
